          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %em causally local net of observables
            is a system (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-presheaf"} co-presheaf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            assigned to regions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
            is
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            = succeed "-commute," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson
          %p
            (Beware that the meaning of “net” here is vaguely similar to, but different from, the concept of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
            (as in: generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            of points) as used in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
            the structure of the local net of quantum observables is used as the very
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatization
            of what a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            actually is (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"}> Haag-Kastler axioms
            ”). This may be thought of as a formalization of a spacetime-localized form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (or rather the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction+picture"} interaction picture
            in the case of
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            = succeed "-propagation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
          %p
            Traditionally local nets of observables are assumed to take values in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            structure on the algebras, and makes sense more generally.
          %p
            In particular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            made precise via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
            are taken to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series+algebras"} formal power series algebras
            (reflecting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            nature of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %em derives
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causal additivity
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            that the perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            form a local net of formal power series algebras (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#CausalLocality"} S-matrix – Causal locality and Quantum observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %p
            Other variants may be considered. For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            one generalizes from observables associated with regions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            to observables associated with more general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+AQFT"} locally covariant perturbative AQFT
          %p
            Moreover, if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            with nontrivial global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            configurations is to be considered (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"}> instantons
            ) then one may show that one needs to consider some kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            local nets of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebras"} homotopical algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} homotopical algebraic quantum field theory
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In the literature there is a certain variance and flexibility of what precisely the axioms on a local net of observables are, though the core aspects are always the same: it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copresheaf"} copresheaf
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            s) on pieces of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            such that algebras assigned to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causally disconnected
            regions commute inside the algebra assigned to any joint neighbourhood.
          %p
            Historically this was first formulated for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            only, where it is known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen"} BrunettiFredenhagen
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p We give the modern general formulation first, and then comment on its restriction to special situations.
          %h3#basic_general_definition Basic general definition
          #CatOfSpacetimeEmbeddings.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LorSp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LorSp
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              whose
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"}> object
                  s are
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                  manifolds; (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"}> Lorentzian manifold
                  s equipped with a time-orientation);
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                  are
                  %em causal
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometric
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedding"} embedding
            %p
              Here we say a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \hookrightarrow Y
              is a
              %strong causal embedding
              if for every two points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1,x_2 \in X
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1)
              is in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/future"} future
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_2)
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1
              is in the future of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg
            for a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi inc
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Alg_{inc} \hookrightarrow Alg
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            on the
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong causally local net of observables
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo :
                    %mi LorSp
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi inc
                    %mo →
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A} : LorSp \to Alg_{inc} \to Alg
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"}> causal locality
                ) whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \coprod X_2 \hookrightarrow X
                is a causal embedding, def.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CatOfSpacetimeEmbeddings"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(X_1) \subset \mathcal{A}(X)
                commutes with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊂
                        %mi 𝒜
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(X_2) \subset \mathcal{A}(X)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} locality
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
              encodes the the physical property known as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-causality"} Einstein-causality
              or
              = succeed "," do
                %em micro-causality
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Many
              %em auxiliary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} operators
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              do
              %em not
              satisfy causal locality: for instance operators associate to
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current"} current
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
              do satisfy the axiom, however, i.e. in particular those that are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetry"} gauge invariant
          %h3#extra_axioms Extra axioms
          %h4#EinsteinLocalities Einstein locality
          %p
            Commutativity of spacelike separated observables can be argued to capture only part of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
          %p
            A natural stronger requirement is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacelike"} spacelike
            separated regions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            are literally
            %a{:href => "quantum%20mechanics#Subsystems"} independent quantum subsystems
            of any larger region. By the formalization of
            %em independent subsystem
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            this means the following:
          #EinsteinLocality.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A local net
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              satisfies
              %strong Einstein locality
              if for every causal embedding
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \coprod X_2 \to X
              the
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum%20mechanics#Subsystems"} subsystems
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A}(X_1) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}(X)
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A}(X_2) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{A}(X)
            %p
              are
              %em independent
              in that the algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(X_1) \vee \mathcal{A}(X_2) \in \mathcal{A}(X)
              which they generate is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}(X_1) \otimes \mathcal{A}(X_2)
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen"}> BrunettiFredenhagen, 5.3.1, axiom 4
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              A local net is Einstein local precisely if it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi LorSp
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A} : (LorSp, \coprod) \to (Alg, \otimes)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#BrunettiFredenhagen"}> BrunettiFredenhagen, 5.3.1, theorem 1
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Einstein locality implies
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              Other properties implied by Einstein locality are sometimes extracted as separate axioms. For instance the condition that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \coprod X_2 \to X
              a causal embedding, we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A}(X_1) \cap \mathcal{A}(X_2) = \mathbb{C}
                    \\,.
          %h4#StrongLocality Strong locality
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11
            ) the following variant of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causal locality
            was considered and shown to be equivalent to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            condition for the system of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            associated with a local net of observables
          #StrongLocality.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  strong causal locality
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/strong+causal+locality"} ?
                )
            %p
              A net of observables is
              %strong
                %span.newWikiWord
                  strongly (causally) local
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/strong+causal+locality"} ?
              if it is microlocal (i.e.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"} causally local
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 = A(O_1)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_2 = A(O_2)
              associated with spacelike separated regions commute with each other, and in addition for all commutative subalgebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \subset A_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2 \subset A_2
              the algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∨
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1 \vee C_2 \subset A(O_1 \vee O_2)
              satisfies
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∨
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_1 \vee C_2) \cap A_1 = C_1
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∨
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∩
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_1 \vee C_2) \cap A_2 = C_2
                  \.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Nuiten11"}> Nuiten 11, def. 14
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              It is clear that Einstein locality implies strong locality, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EinsteinLocality"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Einstein
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi locality
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Strong
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi locality
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Einstein\;locality \;\;\Rightarrow  \;\; Strong\;locality
                    \\,.
            %p
              In fact strong locality is strictly weaker than Einstein locality in that there are strongly locally embedded subalgebras which are not Einstein locally embedded. More discussion of this is in (
              %a{:href => "#Wolters13"}> Wolters 13, section 6.3.3
              ).
          %h4#timeslice_axiom Time-slice axiom
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A local net is said to satisfy the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+slice+axiom"} time slice axiom
              if whenever
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    i : X_1 \to X_2
            %p
              is a causal embedding of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic"} globally hyperbolic
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
              contains a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_2
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{A}(i) : \mathcal{A}(X_1) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                    \\mathcal{A}(X_2)
            %p
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %h4#duality Duality
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+net+of+von+Neumann+algebras"} dual net of von Neumann algebras
          %h4#positive_energy_condition Positive energy condition
          %p (…)
          %h4#spectrum_condition Spectrum condition
          %p (…)
          %h3#special_cases_and_variants Special cases and variants
          %h4#minkowski_nets__vacuum_representation Minkowski nets / Vacuum representation
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+vacuum+representation"} Haag-Kastler vacuum representation
          %h4#conformal_nets Conformal nets
          %p
            The notion of local net in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            see for instance (
            %a{:href => "#BarGinoux11"}> Bär-Ginoux 11
            )
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"}> local quantum field theory
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+locality"}> causal locality
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+locality"} Einstein locality
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
                    %li
                      %p
                        %strong local net of observables
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net+of+C-star+systems"} net of C-star systems
                            (
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+group"}> global gauge group
                            ,
                            = succeed ")" do
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+net"} field net
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time+slice+axiom"}> time slice axiom
                            ,
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+property"} split property
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+of+local+net+of+observables"} cohomology of local net of observables
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra+of+observables"}> factorization algebra of observables
                            ,
                            = succeed "," do
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+algebra"} chiral algebra
                            = succeed "," do
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
                            = succeed "." do
                              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+complex"} blob complex
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
          %h2#References References
          %p
            For historical references see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %p In particular:
          %p
            Early suggestion of a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            of local nets of observables with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-categories"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-categories
            (cf. history recalled in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "strict+omega-category#Street09"} Street (2010), p. 9-10
          %ul
            %li#Roberts79
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+E.+Roberts"}> John E. Roberts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical Aspects of Local Cohomology
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algèbres d’opérateurs et leurs applications en physique mathématique
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 274
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://dmitripavlov.org/scans/roberts-mathematical-aspects-of-local-cohomology.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Roberts-LocalCohomology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %h3#general General
          %p The axioms of local nets on general spacetimes were first articulated in
          %ul#BrunettiFredenhagen
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em Quantum Field Theory on Curved Backgrounds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2063"}> arXiv:0901.2063
              )
          %p A comprehensive review, with plenty of background information, is in
          %ul
            %li#BaerFredenhagen
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+B%C3%A4r"}> Christian Bär
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em Quantum field theory on curved spacetime
              , Lecture notes in physics, Springer (2009)
          %p
            Discussion of Einstein locality of a net of observables equivalently as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            condition on the system of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+toposes"} Bohr toposes
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Nuiten11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Nuiten"}> Joost Nuiten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/bachelor+thesis+Nuiten"} Bohrification of local nets of observables
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://qpl.science.ru.nl/"} QPL 2011
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://rvg.web.cse.unsw.edu.au/eptcs/content.cgi?QPL2011"} EPTCS 95, 2012
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1109.1397"} arXiv:1109.1397
          %p A review of this with some further discussion is in section 6 of
          %ul
            %li#Wolters13
              Sander Wolters,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum toposophy
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ru.nl/~landsman/Wolters.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion of properly co-stacky nets of local observables in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#BeniniSchenkelSzabo15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"}> Marco Benini
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Szabo"} Richard Szabo
              %em Homotopy colimits and global observables in Abelian gauge theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1503.08839"}> arXiv:1503.08839
              )
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            for local nets of observables in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            is considered in
          %ul
            %li#BeniniSchenkelWoike17
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Benini"}> Marco Benini
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schenkel"} Alexander Schenkel
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lukas+Woike"} Lukas Woike
              %em Operads for algebraic quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.08657"}> arXiv:1709.08657
              )
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boardman-Vogt+resolution"} Boardman-Vogt resolution
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
            (
            %a{:href => "#BeniniSchenkelWoike17"}> Benini-Schenkel-Woike 17
            ), lifting it to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+AQFT"} homotopy AQFT
          %ul
            %li#Yau18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Donald+Yau"}> Donald Yau
              ,
              %em Homotopical Quantum Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08101"}> arXiv:1802.08101
              )
          %h3#examples_2 Examples
          %p Discussion of standard example includes
          %ul
            %li#BarGinoux11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+B%C3%A4r"}> Christian Bär
              , N. Ginoux,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Classical and quantum fields on lorentzian manifolds
              = succeed "," do
                %em Global Differential Geometry
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1104.1158"} arXiv:1104.1158
          %h3#in_perturbation_theory In perturbation theory
          %p
            The observation that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} Stückelberg-Bogoliubov-Epstein-Glaser
            local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrices
            yield a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            was first made in
          %ul
            %li
              V. Il’in, D. Slavnov,
              %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
              Theor. Math. Phys.
              %strong 36
              , 32 (1978) (
              %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/135575"}> spire
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01035870"} doi
          %p which was however mostly ignored and forgotten. It is taken up again in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
              Commun.Math.Phys.208:623-661 (2000) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"}> arXiv
              )
          %p (a quick survey is in section 8, details are in section 2).
          %p
            For more on this see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pAQFT"} pAQFT
